# Line endings are not cosmetic here.
#
# This project is developed on Windows, where git's default `core.autocrlf=true`
# rewrites working-tree files to CRLF on checkout. For a shell script that is
# fatal rather than untidy: Git bash runs `build.sh` and fails on the first line
# with `\r: command not found`, and the build scripts under tools/ are how every
# wasm artefact in this repo was produced.
#
# So: everything is stored and checked out with LF.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, never touched.
*.wasm   binary
*.data   binary
*.ttf    binary
*.png    binary
*.tar.gz binary

# Reference transcripts are diffed byte-for-byte against runs on real norns
# hardware (E0-17b). A line-ending rewrite would make every line differ.
tools/screen/fp-*.txt     -text
spike/probe/runs/*.txt    -text

# =========================================================================
# E9-78 — WHAT GOES OUT, declared here and nowhere else.
#
# `git archive HEAD` is how the published tree is cut (see the publishing
# runbook, which is itself one of the things that stays behind), and it
# honours `export-ignore`. So this file is the whole of the decision: the
# repository keeps everything, and the publish is a filter rather than a
# second copy somebody has to maintain.
#
# THE BAR IS THE AGPL, not taste. Section 13 obliges an offer of the
# CORRESPONDING SOURCE — what is needed to build and run the thing being
# served. That is `src/`, `public/`, `patches/`, `tools/` — the wasm
# builds included — and the files that say which upstream commits
# they apply to. Everything below is working material: true, useful, and
# not part of what anybody was handed.
#
# THREE THINGS MOVED TO THE ROOT so they could stay while `docs/` goes:
# `LICENCES.md`, `PINS.md` and `PORTABLE.md`. PINS is not optional — a patch
# without the commit it applies to is not source, it is a riddle. PORTABLE
# moved in E9-129 for a harder reason: `tools/build-portable.mjs` COPIES it
# into the portable archive as its README, so a published tree without it
# cannot build the desktop application at all, and a source that cannot
# build the binary it accompanies is not corresponding source.
#
# `test/` STAYS, which is a judgement and not an obligation. 1055 tests are
# the only evidence for every number in the README, and a clone where
# `npm test` does nothing is a clone that cannot check a word of it.
# Delete the line to change that.

docs/           export-ignore
research/       export-ignore
.claude/        export-ignore

# E9-85 — `desktop/` and `electron-builder.yml` were export-ignored and are
# not any more. The Windows app is PUBLISHED now, and a conveyed binary is
# §6 of the AGPL rather than §13: the source has to go with it. The shell
# is four files; the page it wraps is the same `src/` as everything else.

# E9-92 — `spike/` IS NOW ALL SPIKE, so it goes whole.
#
# The three that were not — the sclang, screen and pack builds — moved to
# `tools/`, where everything else written after the project had a shape
# already lived. They were born in `spike/` in the first commit, answering
# whether SuperCollider could be compiled to wasm at all, and became the
# build without ever being moved. One line here instead of thirteen.
spike/          export-ignore

# What came with them, measured rather than assumed: ELEVEN of those files
# were nearly left behind as “test harnesses”, and two of them —
# `tools/screen/fingerprint.c` and `screen-test.c` — are COMPILED by
# `build-cairo.sh`, `build-freetype-2104.sh` and `build-pixman-0400.sh`.
# Excluding them would have published three build scripts that cannot run.
#
# The other nine are named by those same scripts in the lines they print
# when they finish: `run.mjs`, `fingerprint.mjs`, `osc-test.mjs`,
# `boot-test.mjs`, the `trace/` sources `trace-pixman.sh` links, and the
# `fp-*.txt` transcripts the builds tell you to diff their output against.
# A reference fingerprint is the measurement; without it the build is only
# a claim. So the whole of `tools/` goes, and the one real exclusion is the
# dated run logs — the transcript of a build that happened here once.
tools/**/*.log              export-ignore

